```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Convert markdown to HTML using a suitable library
    // (e.g., cpp_markdown or a custom parser)
    std::string html;
    std::stringstream ss(markdown);
    html << ss.rdbuf();
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a markdown text.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a `std::stringstream` to read the markdown text and convert it to an HTML string. The `rdbuf()` method is used to get the current buffer of the stream, which contains the HTML text.

Note that this is a simple example and may not handle all cases of markdown syntax. For a more robust solution, you may want to use a specialized library like `cpp_markdown` or `markdown-cpp-parser` to parse the markdown text and convert it to HTML.

